ITEMS = [
("A FortiGate user account's type is set to LDAP. Where is the user's password checked?",
 ["On the LDAP server", "In the FortiGate config", "On FortiGuard servers", "In the user group"], "A",
 "A remote user type stores only the username on the FortiGate and sends the password to the named LDAP server. Local users keep their password in the FortiGate configuration."),
("In an LDAP server object, what does the Distinguished Name field set?",
 ["The attribute holding usernames", "Where the user search starts", "The account used to bind", "The group a user must join"], "B",
 "The Distinguished Name is the base of the directory search for users. The Common Name Identifier, such as sAMAccountName, is the attribute that holds the username."),
("An LDAP server object uses the Regular bind type. What else must be entered?",
 ["The server's CA certificate", "A shared RADIUS secret", "A bind username and password", "A FortiToken serial number"], "C",
 "Regular bind logs in to the directory with a service account before searching for the user, so it needs that account's DN and password. Simple bind uses only the user's own credentials."),
("What must match on FortiGate and the RADIUS server for authentication to work?",
 ["The admin password", "The LDAP base DN", "The NTP server", "The shared secret"], "D",
 "RADIUS messages are protected with a shared secret configured on both sides. The server must also list the FortiGate as a client."),
("Users must log in before their traffic matches a policy, and the FortiGate prompts them in the browser. What is this called?",
 ["Active authentication", "Passive authentication", "Device detection", "Agentless polling"], "A",
 "Active authentication asks the user for credentials. Passive methods such as FSSO identify the user from another system without prompting."),
("Which method identifies users from their Windows domain logon without prompting them?",
 ["Captive portal", "FSSO", "Local user login", "Two-factor login"], "B",
 "Fortinet Single Sign-On learns user logons from domain controllers and passes user and group to the FortiGate. Captive portal and local user login both prompt."),
("In FSSO DC agent mode, where is the DC agent installed?",
 ["On every workstation", "On the FortiGate", "On each domain controller", "On the DNS server"], "C",
 "The DC agent runs on every domain controller and reports logon events to the collector agent. Missing a DC means missing the logons that DC handles."),
("Which FSSO mode has the FortiGate itself poll the domain controllers, with no collector installed?",
 ["DC agent mode", "Collector agent polling", "RADIUS single sign-on", "Agentless polling"], "D",
 "In agentless polling mode the FortiGate reads the domain controllers' security event logs itself. It suits small sites and supports fewer features than a collector."),
("In FSSO DC agent mode, where do the DC agents send logon events?",
 ["To the collector agent", "Straight to the FortiGate", "To FortiAnalyzer", "To the RADIUS server"], "A",
 "DC agents forward logon events to the collector agent, which tracks users and groups and passes them to the FortiGate. The FortiGate then matches users to policies."),
("Firewall authentication uses the idle timeout type by default. What is the default timeout?",
 ["1 minute", "5 minutes", "30 minutes", "8 hours"], "B",
 "The auth-timeout default is 5 minutes, counted as idle time. A user with no traffic for that long must authenticate again."),
("Users must enter one-time codes from their phones when they log in through the firewall. What is added to their accounts?",
 ["A RADIUS attribute", "A PKI certificate", "FortiToken Mobile", "An LDAP group"], "C",
 "Assigning a FortiToken Mobile token to a user adds a time-based code to the password check. A user group alone does not add a second factor."),
("What does a captive portal on an interface do for unauthenticated users?",
 ["Blocks them with no notice", "Puts them in a new VLAN", "Logs them in as guests", "Sends them to a login page"], "D",
 "A captive portal redirects unauthenticated web traffic to a login page, then lets the user through after authentication. Policies then match the authenticated user."),
]
assert len(ITEMS) == 12
for i, it in enumerate(ITEMS):
    assert it[2] == "ABCD"[i % 4], i
